 ],
 "literal": "Like ant-holes, with many small water-pools, / climbing on rocks like blacksmith's anvil stones, / where the hunters with curved bows sharpen their arrows, / 'the path where his chariot went has forks,' they say; / without feeling that distress, / this noisy town of strangers reproaches me.",
 "translation": "\"The path where his chariot went is full of forks,\" they say, where curved-bow hunters climb on rocks hot as blacksmiths' anvils to sharpen their arrows, near many small water-pools as tiny as ant-holes. Without understanding that distress, this noisy town of strangers reproaches me.",
 "modern": "எறும்புப் புற்றுகளைப் போலப் பல சிறிய நீர்ச்சுனைகளைக் கொண்டதும், கொல்லனின் உலைக்கல்லைப் போன்ற பாறைகளின் மேல் ஏறி வளைந்த வில்லை உடைய எயினர்கள் தம் அம்புகளைக் கூர்மையாக்கும் பிரியும் வழிகளைக் கொண்டதுமான பாதையில்தான் அவருடைய தேர் சென்றது என்று கூறுவர். அந்தத் துன்பத்தை உணர்ந்து கொள்ளாமல், அயலாரான இவ்வூர்க்காரர்கள் என்னை வீணாகப் பழித்துப் பேசுகின்றனர்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend about the gossip of the townspeople. Her lover has departed through the harsh desert path (pālai) to earn wealth, and the townspeople blame her for her pining. She laments that they do not understand the terrifying nature of the path he has taken. The harshness of the desert is illustrated by the rocks being as hot as a blacksmith's anvil (ulaikkal) and the presence of fierce hunters (eyinar) who sharpen their arrows on them. The water-pools (cuṉai) are dried up, as small as ant-holes (eṟumpi aḷai). The key words 'ulaikkal' (anvil) and 'aḻuṅkal ūr' (noisy town) highlight the contrast between the hero's physical suffering and the heroine's social distress.",
